  "worked_solution": "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot \u0394x\n$$\n\nwhere $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+k\u0394x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\u0394x=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,\u27f9\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$",
